WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Permission-aware RAG is a full-system design practice.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.000
It begins with classifying source data and understanding who is allowed to use it.

3
00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:19.900
Ingestion should capture origin, owner, tenant, classification, ACLs, lifecycle state, update time, and deletion status.

4
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:26.160
Chunking and indexing should preserve those references so searchable records do not lose their permission context.

5
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:33.960
At request time, the system needs trusted identity, tenant, role, group, project, and service context.

6
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:39.180
Retrieval should authorize candidates before any source text enters the model context.

7
00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.600
Deny-by-default behavior protects records with missing or ambiguous metadata.

8
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:48.660
Ranking should work only within the set of allowed candidates, never as a way around policy.

9
00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:52.380
Generation-time controls should preserve the same boundary.

10
00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.240
Retrieved content is evidence, not a policy override.

11
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:09.860
Responses should summarize or cite only sources the user may access, handle conflicting sources carefully, and avoid exposing sensitive fragments through formatting, citations, exports, or logs.

12
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:20.040
Tenant isolation should cover indexes, caches, prompts, transcripts, admin tools, analytics, and backups.

13
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:31.680
Durable safety comes from repeatable engineering and operations: test realistic users, revoked access, stale indexes, deleted documents, missing metadata, and cross-tenant cases.

14
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:37.460
Audit enough context to investigate decisions without over-collecting sensitive text.

15
00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:43.400
Review the system as data, policies, users, and source repositories change.
